# Architecture Boundaries

This rule does not authorize Memnara implementation.

Preserve separation between these layers. Do not collapse them or create circular dependencies:

- AI agent core
- model providers
- runtime backends (emulator adapters today; native PC capture/input later)
- game adapters (optional per title)
- vision / perception
- RAM / state readers (optional; not required for generic play)
- perception fusion
- action / control
- memory
- personality / emotion
- voice
- conversation
- manual-control ownership
- UI
- runtime / generated data

## Required isolation

- Title-specific logic stays in a local GameAdapter, never in the generic agent core.
- Emulator-specific logic stays behind emulator abstractions. Native PC logic, when authorized, stays behind a native runtime abstraction. The core must not assume ROM, Game Boy, emulator, or RAM.
- Runtimes advertise capabilities. The core must not infer capabilities from runtime type alone.
- Model providers must remain replaceable. Do not couple the brain to one vendor or one model.
- Vision and RAM remain labeled as separate sources. Fusion may combine them; it must not hide the source. RAM may be absent.
- Runtime and generated data belong in `D:\Memnara-Data`, not in the source tree at `D:\Memnara`, unless explicitly appropriate.
- Cross-game **and** cross-runtime architecture are first-class. New games add integrations; new platforms add backends. Neither rewrites the agent core.
- VISION + INPUT is the minimum generic gameplay path in the long-term design. A RAM adapter is an enhancement, not a prerequisite.
